#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135033 :tutorial/content "Let $V$ be a vector space. Any function $f:V \\times V \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that takes two vectors $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in V$ and returns a real number is an **inner product** on $V,$ denoted $\\langle \\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\rangle,$ if the following axioms are satisfied:\n\n- Linearity in the first argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩ \\\\  & ⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩,\\,\\alpha \\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle\n$$\n\n- Positive definiteness:\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle \\gt 0\\quad \\textrm{for}\\quad  \\mathbf a\\neq  \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0\\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThe concept of an inner product is a generalization of the dot product. It allows us to apply the geometric ideas of length and angle to abstract vector spaces. \n\nNotice that the standard dot product on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ indeed satisfies these axioms. Recall that if $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf v \\in \\mathbb R^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf u, \\mathbf v\\rangle = \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = u_1\\cdot v_1 + u_2\\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n\\cdot v_n .\n$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf v, \\mathbf w \\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb R.$ Let's now check that our three axioms are satisfied. \n\n- We first show that the dot product is linear in the first argument. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨u+v,w⟩ & =(u+v)\\cdot w \\\\  & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =⟨u,w⟩+⟨v,w⟩\\,✓ \\\\ ⟨\\alpha u,v⟩ & =(\\alpha u)\\cdot v \\\\  & =(\\alpha u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\alpha (u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\alpha ⟨u,v⟩\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we show that the dot product is symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨u,v⟩ & =u\\cdot v \\\\  & =v\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨v,u⟩\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we show that the dot product is positive definite:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨u,u⟩ & =u\\cdot u \\\\  & ={u}_{1}{u}_{1}+{u}_{2}{u}_{2}+⋯+{u}_{n}{u}_{n} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{2}+{u}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{u}_{n}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is clearly positive for all $\\mathbf u\\neq\\mathbf 0$ and is zero for $\\mathbf u = \\mathbf 0 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSince the dot product satisfies our three axioms, we conclude that it is an inner product. \n\nFinally, note that since an inner product is both linear in the first argument and symmetric, then it is also linear in the second argument." :tutorial/id #uuid "5640068c-e4b6-5268-9c3b-d2c6f0906725" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3604 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135077 :tutorial/content "The first type of inner product that we will discuss is the **weighted dot product**. \n\nGiven a sequence of positive real numbers $d_1, d_2, \\ldots d_n > 0,$ called \"weights\", the function\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = \\sum_{i=1}^n d_i x_i y_i\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is similar to the dot product, except here, different components are given different weights." :tutorial/id #uuid "97a9d9a1-c4ef-55fb-ab09-32189a73c2ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3992 :tutorial/title "The Weighted Dot Product"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135078 :tutorial/content "The next inner product that we will discuss concerns inner products on polynomials.\n\nGiven a sequence of distinct real numbers ![](images/52/52cae406fa2952a80a1bab7e02d5667b3acda56e4b2456a3dcb6600a91105482.svg) the function\n![](images/d1/d1f9dd085bdf1f3d49f99a26d17d75a4e1bd1ee413fff06bfff4198e4e1ee59d.svg)\ndefines an inner product on ![](images/35/353273654347dcd414aa60782023eff4d676ea7f598a8d2f9c923ee6c4cba811.svg) the vector space of polynomials of degree at most ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg)\n\nLet's consider a few examples:\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/ba/ba47d908e29aa7475933ff0536d610abe1e3752dd165c8cf6f655f83ae48156b.svg)\n![](images/24/24d15f48f890f3bff207460d175db7d16b35fd21a05e1dc3d1103cde1453c531.svg)\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/50/507c62e239664ab41c74f85c0e905c0a84d3da5183991232db789ce8a9d385f1.svg)\n![](images/e3/e3003acc01323f7ff829cab929cec80c6d8a4d340f66642db172f0926533c9c5.svg)\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/c5/c5c6bb73a744c88af37d2cbce6ac9e27957fc77dbcddb4ec6fb04fb02ac1bbc2.svg)\n![](images/5f/5f66a28d825609fe3e9c6dca065cb3f96e6fe0fafccd56ff87d7608fa7cf35af.svg)\n\nNotice that this inner product on ![](images/29/297749fafabb3d9d253c58491c364d113f5579eacda31a07a8b8c792cc9d7777.svg) requires summing ![](images/9f/9fb9bc4dd378568f6cbe7adaa9000da66d51c5eda878e60aea5131718d33ec3d.svg) terms. This is essential to ensure positive definiteness. For example, the function\n![](images/e3/e3003acc01323f7ff829cab929cec80c6d8a4d340f66642db172f0926533c9c5.svg)\nis *not* an inner product on ![](images/68/686b2d83c1abe018b786ecc5f7c624a4932fda35b57d09ca5385a41f8a3bb233.svg) since there exist nonzero polynomials ![](images/aa/aa374f18ad64a3743718af3bbcef010a756b25dae566ccb9413a790a97b72067.svg) such that ![](images/d1/d138796b5b181d78264c70ef5c9b68db4dba9c6603612cca303c87e09924ec2d.svg) which violates the condition ![](images/63/631ddd9e222592497d44379cd2d6b6aac118ff1a0e4c6587347473112aae26c5.svg)\n\nIn general, a nonzero polynomial of degree at most ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) can have at most ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) distinct roots, so vanishing at ![](images/9f/9fb9bc4dd378568f6cbe7adaa9000da66d51c5eda878e60aea5131718d33ec3d.svg) distinct points forces the polynomial to be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "e88c3857-ea1b-58ee-8584-5e787b53f7f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3993 :tutorial/title "An Inner Product of Polynomials in One Variable"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135079 :tutorial/content "Our final inner product is a generalization of what we saw in the previous example. The function\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{a}^{b} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathcal{C}[a,b],$ the vector space of continuous functions on $[a,b].$ \n\nHere, instead of finding the product of the functions at some discrete points and summing them, we sum (integrate) the product of the functions over the whole interval $[a,b].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9d7e7a9f-6893-5af3-b47e-e5ef2ccba9f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3994 :tutorial/title "An Inner Product of Functions in One Variable"}]}
